**Read:** [Reliability-centered maintenance — NASA, September 2008](../sources/Reliability-Centered-Maintenance-NASA.lingua#r_08ZD3ATSYCJX5586EYSY0M4N3R), maintenance data and feedback; revisit the shared measurement uncertainty Records.

Under a homogeneous Poisson model with failure rate λ and total comparable exposure E, observing no failures has probability exp(−λE). The one-sided 95% upper bound obtained from this observation is λu=−ln(0.05)/E, approximately 2.995732/E. With 10,000 equipment-hours and no failures, this gives about 0.000299573 h⁻¹ under those assumptions, not a demonstrated zero rate.

Pooling hours is meaningful only when failure definitions, conditions and observation are comparable. An idle protective function may not reveal its failure until tested; time since the last successful test is not automatically known successful exposure. Retired, repaired and unobserved units can produce censoring and selection effects.

**Work:** Derive the bound from the likelihood. Construct two datasets with the same equipment-hours but different evidence quality. Explain why demand failures, aging and hidden failures may require different statistical models and why a confidence statement is conditional on that choice.

